[Chapter "Key ideas: The French pawn chain"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{After 1.e4 e6 2.d4 d5 3.e5, White's pawns on d4 and e5 and Black's on d5 and e6 form a chain. Each chain points at the wing where its side should attack: White's at Black's kingside, Black's at White's queenside. Black has two ways to break it.}
*

[Chapter "Attack the base: ...c5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{d4 is the base of White's chain and e5 its tip. A chain is attacked at its base, so Black strikes at d4 with ...c5 at once. White holds d4 with the c-pawn and then a knight.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 {[%csl Yd4,Ye5] The idea starts from this position.} 3... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.} 4. c3 {[%cal Gc3d4] Guards d4 with a pawn.} 4... Nc6 {A second attacker on d4.} 5. Nf3 {A second defender. If d4 ever falls, the pawn on e5 is cut off and weak.} *

[Chapter "Attack the tip: ...f6"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Black's second break hits the tip of the chain with ...f6. This position is from the Tarrasch, after ...c5 and ...cxd4. After the trade on f6, the f-file is half-open for Black's rook.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. Bd3 c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Ne2 cxd4 8. cxd4 {The idea starts from this position.} 8... f6 {[%cal Gf6e5] Hits e5.} 9. exf6 {White gives up the tip.} 9... Nxf6 {[%cal Gf8f1] Black's pieces come out freely, and the f-file is half-open for Black's rook.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans in the Advance"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{In the Advance main line, 3...c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nf3 Qb6, Black's c-pawn, knight and queen all hit d4. White holds it and plays 6.a3 to prepare b4. Each board shows one plan.}
*

[Chapter "White: a3 and b4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{White wants b4, to drive Black's c-pawn away from d4 and gain space on the queenside. Then Bb2 adds another defender of d4.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 {[%cal Ga2a3,Gb2b4] The idea starts from this position.} 6. a3 {Prepares b4.} 6... Bd7 7. b4 {[%cal Gb4c5] Gains space and asks the c-pawn to decide.} 7... cxd4 8. cxd4 {No White pawn guards d4 now, so White uses pieces.} 8... Rc8 9. Bb2 {[%cal Gb2d4] A third defender of d4, with the knight and queen.} *

[Chapter "Black: ...c4 and the hole on b3"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{With ...c4 Black takes away White's b4 plan: b4 would now be met by ...cxb3 en passant. The square b3 becomes a hole for Black's knight, and White has to guard it.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 {[%csl Yb3] The idea starts from this position.} 6... c4 {[%cal Rb2b4] Now b4 is met by ...cxb3 en passant.} 7. Nbd2 {Guards b3.} 7... Na5 {[%cal Ga5b3] The knight eyes b3.} 8. Rb1 {Guards b2, so the c1 bishop can move, and prepares b3 to challenge the c4 pawn.} 8... Bd7 {Black develops and later castles queenside, then breaks with ...f6.} *

[Chapter "Don't jump into b3 too early"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{The knight jump to b3 looks strong, but White can simply take it. After the trades the pawn that lands on b3 has no support.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 c4 7. Nbd2 Na5 8. Rb1 {The idea starts from this position.} 8... Nb3? {Looks active, but it drops a pawn.} 9. Nxb3 Qxb3 10. Qxb3 cxb3 11. Nd2 {[%csl Rb3] The pawn on b3 falls. Stockfish +1.8, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "White holds d4 with pieces"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{If Black takes on d4 instead, no White pawn can guard d4 any more. White guards it with pieces and uses the knight to chase Black's queen. The square c5 becomes a weak square in Black's camp.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 cxd4 7. cxd4 {The idea starts from this position.} 7... Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] Heads for f5, to hit d4 again.} 8. Nc3 Nf5 9. Na4 {[%cal Ga4b6,Ga4c5] Hits the queen and eyes c5.} 9... Qd8 10. b4 {[%cal Gc1b2] Space on the queenside, and Bb2 comes next. Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: The Winawer structure"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{After 3.Nc3 Bb4 4.e5 c5 5.a3 Bxc3+ 6.bxc3, White has doubled c-pawns but the two bishops, and the pawn on c3 supports d4. Black has given up the bishop that guarded the dark squares. Each board shows what that means.}
*

[Chapter "The weak g7 pawn"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{Without its dark-squared bishop, nothing guards Black's g7 pawn. That is why White's queen goes straight to g4.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 {[%csl Yg7] The idea starts from this position.} 6... Ne7 {[%cal Ge7f5] The e5 pawn covers f6, so the knight develops to e7.} 7. Qg4 {[%cal Gg4g7] Attacks g7 at once.} *

[Chapter "The Poisoned Pawn"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "both"]

{Black lets g7 go and takes White's center pawns instead. White wins two pawns on the kingside, Black attacks c3 and e5, and Black's king stays in the center behind its pawns.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Ne7 7. Qg4 {The idea starts from this position.} 7... Qc7 {[%cal Gc7c3] Pressure on c3 and e5.} 8. Qxg7 Rg8 9. Qxh7 cxd4 {[%cal Gc7c3] White can't take back: 10.cxd4? Qc3+ forks the king and rook.} 10. Ne2 {Guards c3.} *

[Chapter "The safe choice: 7...O-O"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Castling guards g7 without giving up a pawn. It is solid, but Black has to defend while White aims at h7.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Ne7 7. Qg4 {The idea starts from this position.} 7... O-O {Guards g7.} 8. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3h7] Aims at h7.} 8... Nbc6 9. Qh5 {[%cal Gh5h7] A second attacker on h7.} 9... Ng6 {The knight shields the kingside.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: The ...f6 break and the e6 pawn"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{In the Tarrasch and the Steinitz, Black hits d4 with ...c5 and then the tip of the chain with ...f6. After the trade on f6, Black's pieces come out freely, but the e6 pawn is backward and e5 is a square for White's pieces. In the Steinitz, Black usually plays on the queenside first.}
*

[Chapter "The knight jump to e4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{After ...f6 and exf6 Nxf6 in the Tarrasch, the square e4 is a strong post for Black's knight, and the rook on f8 now hits the knight on f4. White can't simply take on e4: 14.Bxe4? dxe4 attacks the knight on f3, and Black wins material (Stockfish −4.5).}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. Bd3 c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Ne2 cxd4 8. cxd4 f6 9. exf6 Nxf6 10. O-O Bd6 11. Nf3 O-O 12. Bf4 Bxf4 13. Nxf4 {[%csl Ye4,Ye5,Ye6] The idea starts from this position.} 13... Ne4 {[%cal Gf8f4] Strong post, and the rook hits f4.} 14. Qc1 {Guards the knight on f4.} 14... Ng5 15. Nxg5 Qxg5 {Black's pieces are active. Equal (Stockfish +0.2).} *

[Chapter "The Steinitz: queenside first"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{In the Steinitz, the pawn on f4 holds e5, so ...f6 has less effect. Black expands on the queenside first with ...a6 and ...b5, and trades off White's dark-squared bishop.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yf4,Ye5] The idea starts from this position.} 7... a6 {Prepares ...b5.} 8. Qd2 b5 {[%cal Gb5b4] Space on the queenside.} 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. Bxc5 Nxc5 {A good knight on c5. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Open games: Exchange and Rubinstein"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{After 3.exd5 exd5 or 3...dxe4, the center opens and the pawn chain is gone. Development and the open files matter most. Each board shows one idea.}
*

[Chapter "Exchange: the e-file and ...Nge7"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "both"]

{The pawns are symmetrical and the e-file is open; the side with the more active pieces controls it and the squares e4 and e5. Black's knight goes to e7, not f6, so the f-pawn stays free to move and the knight can come to f5 or g6.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Bd3 Bg4 6. c3 Bd6 7. O-O Qd7 {[%csl Ye4,Ye5] The idea starts from this position.} 8. Re1+ {[%cal Ge1e8] The rook takes the open file with check.} 8... Nge7 {Blocks the check. The knight is pinned for one move, until Black castles.} 9. Nbd2 O-O {Black castles short.} *

[Chapter "Rubinstein: ...c5 frees the game"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{After 3...dxe4, Black's d-pawn has taken White's e-pawn, so the center is White's d4 against Black's e6. Black frees the game with ...c5, which hits d4 and opens lines for the bishops.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Bd3 Nxe4 7. Bxe4 Nf6 8. Bd3 {[%csl Yd4,Ye6] The idea starts from this position.} 8... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] The freeing break.} 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. O-O O-O {Black has no weaknesses. Equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: The French bishop"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Black's c8 bishop is shut in by its own pawns on e6 and d5, so it is often called the bad French bishop. Handling it well is half the battle for Black; for White, it is a target. Each board shows one way to solve it.}
*

[Chapter "Trade it: ...Bd7 and ...Bb5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{In the Advance, Black can offer to trade the bad bishop for White's good one with ...Bd7 and ...Bb5. This is the Wade Variation. White takes and gains time on the queen.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Qb6 5. Nf3 {[%cal Gc8d7,Gd7b5] The idea starts from this position.} 5... Bd7 6. Be2 Bb5 {[%cal Gb5e2] Offers the trade.} 7. Bxb5+ Qxb5 8. Na3 {[%cal Ga3b5] Gains time on the queen. Stockfish +0.2.} *

[Chapter "Trade it: ...b5 and ...Ba6"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{In the Steinitz, the bishop comes out on a6 to trade itself for White's good bishop. Don't take on d3 with the knight: 12...Nxd3+? 13.cxd3 gives up Black's best piece and leaves the bad bishop at home (Stockfish +0.5).}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 8. Qd2 b5 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. Bxc5 Nxc5 11. Qf2 Qb6 12. Bd3 {[%cal Gc8a6,Ga6d3] The idea starts from this position.} 12... b4 {Chases the knight from c3.} 13. Ne2 a5 14. O-O Ba6 {[%cal Ga6d3] Offers the trade. Equal.} *

[Chapter "The long diagonal"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{In the Rubinstein the bishop can't be trapped by pawns on e6 and d5, because the d-pawn is gone. After ...b6 it comes to b7, on the long diagonal toward White's king.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Bd3 Nxe4 7. Bxe4 Nf6 8. Bd3 c5 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. O-O O-O {[%cal Gc8b7,Gb7g2] The idea starts from this position.} 11. Qe2 b6 12. Bg5 Bb7 {[%cal Gb7g2] The French bishop is solved. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{A few tactical patterns come up again and again in the French. Each board shows one, with the moves that make it work.}
*

[Chapter "Bb5+ in the Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{The pawn on d4 looks free, because White's bishop on d3 blocks the queen's defence. But after the trades on d4, Bb5+ moves the bishop off the d-file with check. Black must play ...Bd7 first.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 {[%cal Rd3b5,Rb5e8] [%csl Rd4] The idea starts from this position.} 7... Nxd4?? {Takes the bait.} 8. Nxd4 Qxd4? 9. Bb5+ {[%cal Gd1d4] Check, and White's queen now sees d4.} 9... Bd7 {Blocking doesn't help.} 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Qxd4 {White has won the queen. Stockfish +7.0, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Leave e5 alone"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{Later in the same gambit, the e5 pawn looks free too. After ...Qxe5, White's rook comes to the e-file with gain of time, and Black's king is still in the center.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 {[%cal Re1e5,Re5e8] The idea starts from this position.} 10... Qxe5? {Greedy.} 11. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5] Hits the queen.} 11... Qd6 12. Nb5 {Hits the queen again. Only 12...Qb6 holds; 12...Bxb5? 13.Bxb5+ and 12...Qb8? 13.Qf3 lose.} *

[Chapter "The queen behind d4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{Count the defenders before taking on d4. Here White's queen still sees d4, so taking the pawn leaves Black a piece down.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 cxd4 7. cxd4 {[%cal Gd1d4] The idea starts from this position.} 7... Nxd4? 8. Nxd4 {[%cal Gd1d4] Now 8...Qxd4?? 9.Qxd4 loses the queen, so Black is simply a knight down for a pawn. Stockfish +3.6, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Qh5+ against an early ...f6"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{Breaking with ...f6 too early loosens the diagonal from h5 to Black's king. If Black also takes on e5, the queen check wins material.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 f6 {[%cal Gd1h5,Gh5e8] The idea starts from this position.} 4. Bd3 {Prepares Qh5+.} 4... fxe5? 5. Qh5+ g6 6. Bxg6+ {6...hxg6?? 7.Qxh8 loses the rook.} 6... Kd7 {Black's king is stuck in the center. Stockfish +1.7, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Qh4+ against 3.f3"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{The move f3 weakens the diagonal from h4 to White's king. After 4.fxe4?, the queen check wins the e4 pawn.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. f3?! {[%cal Gd8h4,Gh4e1] The idea starts from this position.} 3... dxe4 4. fxe4? Qh4+ {[%cal Gh4e4] Checks and hits e4.} 5. g3? Qxe4+ {Forks the king and the rook on h1. Stockfish −4.0, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 3.e5"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "3...c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Main Line"]
[ECO "C02"]

{White gains space with 3.e5, and Black hits the base of the chain at once with ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6. In this main line White plays 6.a3 to prepare b4, and Black stops it for good with 6...c4. The center is then locked: Black plays on the queenside and later breaks with ...f6, while White plays on the kingside. Black's other answers to 6.a3 and White's other sixth moves are under Advanced; the Milner-Barry Gambit, 6.Bd3, is under Traps.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 {The Advance. White gains space and keeps Black's king's knight off f6.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4, the base of White's chain, at once.}
4. c3 {Guards d4 with a pawn, so White can always recapture there with the c-pawn. 4.Nf3 Nc6 5.c3 Qb6 reaches the same position.}
4... Nc6 {Adds a second attacker on d4.}
5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack. A second defender of d4.} Qb6 {[%cal Gb6d4,Gb6b2] A third attacker on d4. The queen also eyes b2, which matters once White's c1 bishop leaves.}
6. a3 {The main line. White prepares b4, to drive the c-pawn away from d4 and gain space on the queenside.} c4 {[%cal Rb2b4] [%csl Yb3] Stops b4 for good. Black gives up the pressure on d4 but fixes White's queenside, and b3 becomes a hole. 6...cxd4 is more common at club level; it is under Advanced.}
7. Nbd2 {Guards b3. After 7.Be2 Na5 8.Nbd2, play the same way.} Na5 {[%cal Ga5b3] The knight eyes b3. Don't jump in yet: 8...Nb3? 9.Nxb3 Qxb3 10.Qxb3 cxb3 loses the pawn on b3 to 11.Nd2.}
8. Be2 {After 8.Rb1 Bd7 9.Be2, the play is the same.} Bd7 {Black develops and prepares to castle queenside.}
9. O-O Ne7 {The king's knight heads for c6 or f5. With White's pawn on e5, f6 is out of reach.}
10. Rb1 {Prepares b3 to open the b-file.} h6 {Keeps White's knight off g5 and a bishop off g5.}
11. Re1 O-O-O {Black castles where the pawn on c4 has closed the position.}
12. Nf1 Kb8 {[%cal Gf7f6] Black's plan is ...Nc6 or ...Nf5, then ...f6; White plays on the kingside with Ng3 and Bf4, or breaks with b3. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 3.exd5"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "3...exd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "French Defense: Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "C01"]

{After 3.exd5 exd5 the pawns are symmetrical and the e-file is open. Black develops with ...Nc6, ...Bg4 and ...Bd6, puts the king's knight on e7 so Bg5 can't pin it, and castles short. White keeps the game quiet with c3, castling and Re1. 2.Nf3 d5 3.exd5 exd5 4.d4 reaches the same position; White's other fourth and fifth moves are under Advanced.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4
2... d5 3. exd5 {The Exchange. White releases the tension and frees Black's c8 bishop.} exd5 {Recapture toward the center. The c8 bishop now has an open diagonal.}
4. Nf3 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Develops and hits d4, ready for ...Bg4 to pin the knight that defends it.}
5. Bd3 {The most natural square: the bishop aims at h7.}
5... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3,Gc6d4] Pins the knight on f3, which defends d4.

Watch for White's mistakes here. 6.h3? Bxf3 7.Qxf3 Nxd4 wins the d4 pawn (Stockfish −0.9). 6.O-O also allows 6...Bxf3 7.Qxf3 Nxd4, though White gets some activity for the pawn (Stockfish −0.1). Against 6.Be3, develop with 6...Bd6 and ...Nge7.}
6. c3 {Guards d4 with a pawn, so ...Bxf3 no longer wins it.} Bd6 7. O-O {Against 7.h3, keep the pin with 7...Bh5.} Qd7 {Connects the rooks and supports ...Bf5 or ...Bh3.}
8. Re1+ Nge7 {[%csl Ge7] Blocks the check. On e7 the knight can't be pinned, and later it goes to g6 or f5.}
9. Nbd2 O-O {Castle short. Long castling looks aggressive, but here White's pawn storm with b4 and b5 arrives first (Stockfish +0.7 after 9...O-O-O).}
10. Nf1 Bf5 {Offers to trade White's good bishop.} 11. Bxf5 Qxf5 12. Ng3 Qd7 {Equal (Stockfish +0.1). Black's pieces are active, and the e-file will see trades.} *

[Chapter "Winawer: 3.Nc3 Bb4"]
[Diagram "3...Bb4"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "French Defense: Winawer Variation, Poisoned Pawn Variation, Main Line"]
[ECO "C18"]

{3...Bb4 pins the knight on c3. After 4.e5 c5 5.a3 Bxc3+ 6.bxc3, White has doubled c-pawns but the two bishops and a pawn on c3 that guards d4. In the Poisoned Pawn, 7.Qg4, White takes the pawns on g7 and h7, and Black takes White's center pawns and attacks c3 and e5. Sharp for both sides: Black's king often stays in the center, behind its pawns. The safer 7...O-O and White's other 7th moves are not covered yet.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 {The Winawer. The bishop pins the knight on c3, which defends e4.}
4. e5 {White's most common move: it gains space and takes f6 from Black's knight.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black hits d4 at once. With the knight on c3, White can't support d4 with c3.}
5. a3 {Puts the question to the bishop: take on c3 or retreat to a5.} Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 {[%csl Yc3] White's pawns are doubled, but the pawn on c3 now guards d4, and White has the two bishops.} Ne7 {[%cal Ge7f5] Black develops the knight, since f6 is not available.}
7. Qg4 {[%cal Gg4g7] The Poisoned Pawn: the queen attacks g7, which Black's dark-squared bishop no longer guards.} Qc7 {Black gives up g7 for activity and pressure on c3 and e5. Castling, 7...O-O, is safer but leaves Black defending.}
8. Qxg7 Rg8 9. Qxh7 cxd4 {[%cal Gc7c3,Gd4c3] Black opens the center. White can't take back: 10.cxd4? Qc3+ forks the king and the rook on a1 and wins material (Stockfish −4.0).}
10. Ne2 {The main move: it guards c3 and develops.} Nbc6 11. f4 {Guards e5 and gains space on the kingside.} dxc3
12. Qd3 {The most common move at club level: the queen comes back to blockade and win back c3. The video plays 12.Nxc3 a6.} d4 {[%csl Yc3] Black guards the pawn on c3 and cramps White. Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White, but Black has active pieces and the open g-file.} *

[Chapter "Tarrasch: 3.Nd2"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "3...Nf6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "French Defense: Tarrasch Variation, Closed Variation, Main Line"]
[ECO "C06"]

{3.Nd2 also guards e4, but it keeps the c-pawn free, so White can support d4 with c3 without spoiling the queenside. After 3...Nf6 4.e5 Nfd7, Black attacks the chain with ...c5 and then ...f6. In this main line the f-file opens after 9.exf6 Nxf6: Black gets free piece play and a strong knight on e4, while White aims at e5 and the pawn on e6. Against 3.Nd2, Black can also play 3...c5 (not covered yet) or take on e4, the Rubinstein lesson.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 {The Tarrasch. The knight guards e4 and leaves the c-pawn free.} Nf6 {Attacks e4 and invites e5.}
4. e5 Nfd7 5. Bd3 c5 6. c3 {[%cal Gc3d4] Supports d4 with a pawn, which the knight on d2 makes possible.} Nc6
7. Ne2 {[%cal Gd2f3] The knight goes to e2, not f3: White keeps f4 free, and the other knight can come to f3 to defend d4.} cxd4 8. cxd4 f6 {[%cal Gf6e5] Black hits the head of the chain.}
9. exf6 {The quieter main line. 9.Nf4 is a sharp alternative.} Nxf6 {Black's pieces come out freely, and the f-file is half-open for Black's rook.}
10. O-O Bd6 11. Nf3 O-O 12. Bf4 {Offers to trade Black's good bishop.} Bxf4 13. Nxf4 {[%csl Ye5,Ye6] White controls e5 and eyes the pawn on e6, while Black has the f-file.} Ne4 {[%cal Gf8f4] A strong square for the knight, and the rook on f8 now attacks the knight on f4. 14.Bxe4? dxe4 attacks the knight on f3, and Black wins material (Stockfish −4.5).}
14. Qc1 {Guards the knight on f4.} Ng5 15. Nxg5 Qxg5 {Black's pieces are active, and White has the weaker king position for now. Slightly better for White (Stockfish +0.3).} *

[Chapter "Steinitz: 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.e5"]
[Diagram "3...Nf6"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "French Defense: Classical Variation, Steinitz Variation"]
[ECO "C11"]

{After 3...Nf6, the Steinitz Variation, 4.e5 Nfd7 5.f4, gives White a big center with pawns on d4, e5 and f4. Black hits it with ...c5 and ...Nc6, then expands on the queenside with ...a6, ...b5 and ...b4. d4 is White's main weakness, e6 is Black's, and Black's c8 bishop often comes out to a6 to trade White's good bishop. White's other answer to 3...Nf6, 4.Bg5, is not covered yet.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The knight develops and attacks e4.}
4. e5 {The Steinitz Variation: White gains space and drives the knight back.} Nfd7 {The only good square: on d7 the knight supports both breaks, ...c5 and ...f6.} 5. f4 {[%cal Gf4e5] White supports e5 with a pawn and gains space on the kingside.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black hits d4, the base of the chain.}
6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 {Guards d4 a third time.} a6 {Prepares ...b5 to gain space on the queenside.}
8. Qd2 {Connects the rooks and prepares long castling.} b5 9. dxc5 {Releases the tension before Black's pressure on d4 grows.} Bxc5 10. Bxc5 Nxc5 {Black has traded the dark-squared bishops and has a good knight on c5.}
11. Qf2 {Attacks the knight.} Qb6 12. Bd3 b4 {[%cal Gb5b4] Chases the knight from c3. 12...Nxd3+? 13.cxd3 only strengthens d4 (Stockfish +0.6, against 0.0 after 12...b4).}
13. Ne2 a5 {Black has gained a lot of space on the queenside.} 14. O-O Ba6 {[%cal Ga6d3] Offers to trade Black's bad bishop for White's good one.} 15. Kh1 {Steps off the diagonal of Black's queen. Equal (Stockfish −0.1), and Black has the easier plans on the queenside.} *

[Chapter "Rubinstein: 3.Nc3 dxe4"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "3...dxe4"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "French Defense: Rubinstein Variation, Blackburne Defense"]
[ECO "C10"]

{Black gives up the center with 3...dxe4, against 3.Nc3 and also against 3.Nd2, since 4.Nxe4 reaches the same position. Black gets quick, safe development: ...Nd7 and ...Ngf6 trade White's knight on e4, then ...c5 frees the game. White keeps a little more space. The line is named after Akiba Rubinstein.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 {Defends e4 with the knight.}
3... dxe4 {Takes the pawn. White's knight must spend a move to recapture.}
4. Nxe4 {Against the gambit 4.f3, play 4...Bb4: after 5.fxe4 Qh4+ White's king has to move.} Nd7 {[%cal Gd7f6,Gg8f6] This is the Blackburne Defense of the Rubinstein Variation. Prepares ...Ngf6. If White takes on f6, Black recaptures with the other knight and keeps a sound pawn structure.}
5. Nf3
5... Ngf6 {Challenges the knight on e4.}
6. Bd3 {Keeps the knight in the center.} Nxe4 {Trades White's best piece.}
7. Bxe4 Nf6 {Develops with gain of time on the bishop.}
8. Bd3 {After 8.Bg5, play 8...c5 at once.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] The freeing break. Black attacks d4 and opens lines for the bishops.}
9. dxc5 {After 9.O-O cxd4 10.Nxd4 Bc5, or 9.c3 cxd4 10.Nxd4 Bc5, Black is equally comfortable.} Bxc5 10. O-O O-O 11. Qe2 b6 {[%cal Gc8b7] The c8 bishop goes to b7, on the long diagonal. The French bishop is solved.}
12. Bg5 Bb7 {Equal (Stockfish 0.0). White's pieces look active, but Black has no weaknesses.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 4...Qb6 and 4...cxd4"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...Qb6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Wade Variation"]
[ECO "C02"]

{Black can play ...Qb6 or ...cxd4 before ...Nc6. Most of the time it comes back to the lines you already know, but there are two ideas to learn: the Wade Variation, where Black trades the French bishop with ...Bd7 and ...Bb5, and the check 5...Bb4+, which is a mistake.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Qb6
(4... cxd4 5. cxd4 Nc6
(5... Bb4+? {Checks without a purpose.} 6. Nc3 {Blocks with a developing move.} Nc6 7. Nf3 Bd7 8. Bd3 Nge7 9. O-O {White develops quickly; a3 will drive the bishop back. Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White.})
6. Nf3 {The same position as the 5...cxd4 chapter.})
5. Nf3 Bd7
(5... Nc6 {The main line of the Advance after 5...Qb6.}) {The Wade Variation. Black wants ...Bb5 to trade off the French bishop.}
6. Be2 Bb5 7. Bxb5+ {Trade at once: after ...Qxb5, Na3 hits the queen.} Qxb5 8. Na3 Qd7 9. Nc2 {[%cal Gc2d4,Gc2e3] The knight reroutes to support d4.} cxd4 10. cxd4 Nc6 11. O-O Nge7 {White keeps the space advantage. Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Advance: Black's other sixth moves after 6.a3"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "6...Bd7"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Main Line"]
[ECO "C02"]

{After 6.a3, Black has five other sixth moves. Against 6...Bd7, 6...Nge7 and 6...Nh6, push 7.b4: White takes space on the queenside and gets ready for Bb2. Against 6...a5, play 7.Bd3. 6...c4 is the Advance lesson under Basic.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Qb6 6. a3 Bd7
(6... Nge7 7. b4 cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5 9. Bb2 Bd7 10. g4 {Drives the knight back from f5.} Nfe7 11. Nc3 {Equal (Stockfish 0.0), but White has more space.})
(6... Nh6 {This is the Lputian Variation.} 7. b4 cxd4 8. cxd4 Nf5 9. Bb2 Bd7 10. g4 Nfe7 11. Nc3 {The same plan as against 6...Nge7. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).})
7. b4 {Takes space and drives the c-pawn away from d4.} cxd4 {After 7...cxb4? 8.axb4, the a-file opens for White's rook and Black's pressure on d4 is gone (Stockfish +1.3).}
8. cxd4 Rc8 9. Bb2 {A fourth defender of d4.} Nge7 10. Nc3 Na5 11. Na4 {[%cal Ga4c5] The knight heads for c5.} Qc6 12. Nc5 {White's knight on c5 is a strong outpost. Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 5...Bd7"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Euwe Variation"]
[ECO "C02"]

{GM Igor Smirnov's choice for Black. 5...Bd7 waits, keeps ...Qb6 and ...Rc8 in reserve, and sets two traps for natural White moves. Play 6.Be2 and castle. Black's other sixth moves are under Advanced variations.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Bd7 {[%cal Rc6e5] This is the Euwe Variation. Now a bishop check on b5 would no longer pin the knight on c6, so ...Nxe5 becomes a threat whenever e5 is short of defenders.}
6. Be2 {Safe and solid. Castle next.

6.Bd3 is playable, but Black aims ...Rc8, ...cxd4 and ...Nb4 at the bishop. After 6...Rc8 7.O-O cxd4 8.cxd4 Nb4, give up the bishop with 9.Nc3 Nxd3 10.Qxd3; saving it with 9.Be2? lets 9...Nc2 trap the rook (Stockfish −2.2).}
(6. Bb5? {Looks natural, but the e5 pawn is now weak.} Nxe5! {The knight takes, because the bishop on d7 covers b5.} 7. Nxe5 Bxb5 {Black has won a pawn, and the bishop on b5 stops White from castling. Stockfish −1.7, clearly better for Black.})
6... Qb6 {The most common reply. After 6...Nge7 or 6...cxd4, see Advanced variations.}
7. O-O {Castles first. d4 is protected enough: the knight on f3 and the queen guard it, and ...Nxd4 runs into Nxd4 Qxd4 Qxd4.} cxd4 8. cxd4 Nge7 9. Nc3 Nf5 10. Na4 {Hits the queen.} Qa5 11. b3 {Protects the knight on a4. Next Bb2 and Rc1.} Rc8 {White has more space and a solid center. Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 6.a3 cxd4"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "6...cxd4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Main Line"]
[ECO "C02"]

{After 5...Qb6 6.a3, 6...cxd4 is Black's most common reply at club level. After 7.cxd4 no White pawn can defend d4, so White uses pieces: Nc3 and Na4 chase the queen, and b4 and Bb2 follow. Count the attackers before Black takes on d4: with White's queen behind it, ...Nxd4 drops a piece.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 {[%cal Ge5f6] Gains space and takes f6 away from Black's king's knight.} c5 {Black hits d4, the base of the chain, at once.}
4. c3 {Guards d4 with a pawn, so White can always recapture there with the c-pawn.} Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack. A third defender of d4.} Qb6 {[%cal Rb6d4,Rb6b2] A third attacker. The queen also eyes b2, which is why White's c1 bishop often waits.}
6. a3 {[%cal Gb2b4] Prepares b4. It drives the c-pawn away from d4, gains space on the queenside, and frees b2 for the bishop.} cxd4 {Black releases the tension before b4 comes.}
7. cxd4 {Now no White pawn can defend d4 any more, so White uses pieces: the knight on f3, a bishop on b2 or e3, and the queen.

Count before Black takes. 7...Nxd4? 8.Nxd4 Qxd4 9.Qxd4 just loses a piece, because White's queen sees d4.} Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] The knight heads for f5 to hit d4 again. After 7...Bd7, play 8.Nc3 and Na4 the same way.}
8. Nc3 {Develops and prepares Na4, which hits the queen.} Nf5 9. Na4 {[%cal Ga4b6,Ga4c5] Chases the queen and eyes c5, a hole in Black's camp.} Qd8 {After 9...Qa5+, block with 10.b4!: the pawn hits the queen again (Stockfish +1.2).}
10. b4 {[%cal Gc1b2] Takes space on the queenside and prepares Bb2, a fourth defender of d4.} Bd7 11. Bb2 b6 12. Bd3 Be7 13. O-O {White has more space, d4 is safe, and the knight on a4 keeps Black's queenside busy. Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 5...cxd4"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Paulsen Attack"]
[ECO "C02"]

{Black opens the c-file at once. After 6.cxd4, no White pawn can defend d4, so White develops pieces quickly: Nc3, Bb5 or Bd3, and castles. Black's other sixth moves, including the check 6...Bb4+, are under Advanced variations.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} cxd4 6. cxd4 Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] The knight heads for f5.}
7. Nc3 {Develops and guards e4 and d5. After 7...Nf5, d4 is attacked twice and defended twice (knight and queen).} Nf5
8. Bb5 {Pins the knight on c6, which attacks d4.} Bd7 9. Ba4 {Keeps the pin and prepares Bc2, where the bishop aims at h7.} Be7 10. O-O O-O 11. Bc2 {[%cal Gc2h7] The bishop aims at Black's castled king. Next Bxf5 to win the d4 battle, or Qd3 and Ne2. Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 5...Nge7"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Paulsen Attack"]
[ECO "C02"]

{Black brings the king's knight toward f5 or g6 before deciding about the queen. Play 6.Bd3, which covers f5. If the knight goes to g6, the h-pawn chases it. Black's other sixth moves are under Advanced variations.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Nge7
6. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3f5] Covers f5, the knight's best square.} Ng6 {The most common reply. The knight hits e5 from g6.}
7. h4! {[%cal Gh4h5] Threatens h5, which drives the knight away from e5.} Nge7 {7...h5 weakens g5 and g6.}
8. h5 {Gains space on the kingside. Black's knights have few squares.} Nf5 9. dxc5 {Now White gives up the base of the chain: Black's pieces are not ready to win e5.} Bxc5 10. Qe2 {Guards e5 and prepares b4 or Bf4. Stockfish +1.3, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Advance: Black's other third moves"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "3...Nc6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation"]
[ECO "C02"]

{Black's rarer third moves. Most of them come back to the main lines after ...c5. Against 3...Bd7, play 4.Nf3 and 5.c3. Against 3...f6, see the trap chapter.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Nc6 {The knight blocks Black's c-pawn, so ...c5 is not coming.}
(3... Ne7 4. c3 c5 5. Nf3 Nbc6 {The same position as the 5...Nge7 chapter.})
(3... a6 4. c3 c5 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Bd3 {Develop and castle. Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White.})
4. Nf3 f6 {Black has to break with ...f6, since ...c5 is blocked.} 5. c3 fxe5 6. Nxe5 Nxe5 7. dxe5 Ne7 8. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3h7] White's e5 pawn cramps Black, and the bishop aims at h7. Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 6.Be2 and other sixth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Be2"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Paulsen Attack"]
[ECO "C02"]

{After 5...Qb6, White's three main sixth moves come up about equally often: 6.Bd3 (the Milner-Barry Gambit, under Traps and tricks), 6.a3 (the main line) and 6.Be2. Against 6.Be2, take on d4 and bring the king's knight to f5 via h6. Against the rarer 6.b3, 6.Qb3 or 6.Na3, use the same plan: take on d4 when White can only recapture with the c-pawn, then bring a knight to f5.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Qb6
6. Be2 {Prepares to castle.} cxd4 {Take first. Now no White pawn can ever defend d4 again.}
7. cxd4 Nh6 {[%cal Gh6f5] The knight heads for f5, where it hits d4. On h6 it looks odd, but White can't win it.}
8. Bxh6 {White trades the bishop to double Black's pawns.

Other eighth moves. After 8.O-O Nf5, Black has three pieces on d4. After 8.Nc3 Nf5 9.Na4 Qa5+ 10.Nc3, Black can repeat with 10...Qb6 or play on with 10...Bb4.}
Qxb2 {[%cal Gb2a1] Hits the rook on a1, and the bishop on h6 is still hanging.}
9. Nbd2 {Develops and clears the first rank, so the queen on d1 now guards the rook. Don't fear 9.Bxg7?: 9...Bxg7 10.Nbd2 Nxd4 wins a pawn (Stockfish −0.4).} gxh6 {Black has won the piece back. The pawns on h6 and h7 are doubled, but Black has the bishop pair and the open g-file for a rook.}
10. O-O Bd7 11. Nb3 Rc8 {Black's queen will retreat to b6 or a3. Equal (Stockfish +0.1).} *

[Chapter "Advance: White's other fourth and fifth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.Bb5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation"]
[ECO "C02"]

{White's rarer tries before the main line. Two of them use a bishop check or pin on b5. The answer to both is ...Bd7, and Black is happy to trade the French bishop. Against 5.Be3 or 5.f4, play 5...Qb6 and keep hitting d4 and b2. Against 4.dxc5, take back with 4...Bxc5 or develop with 4...Nc6 first.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5
4. c3
(4. Bb5+ Bd7 {Offers to trade light-squared bishops, a good deal for Black.} 5. Bxd7+ Qxd7 6. c3 Nc6 7. Nf3 Nge7 {Next ...Nf5 hits d4. Black's problem bishop is gone. Slightly better for Black (Stockfish −0.2).})
(4. Nf3 {This is the Nimzowitsch System. Develops first and leaves c3 for later.} Nc6 {A second attacker on d4.} 5. c3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Qb6 {The same position as the Advance main line, by another move order. Against 5.Bb5, play 5...Bd7, and against 5.dxc5, take back with 5...Bxc5.})
4... Nc6 5. Bb5 {Pins the knight that attacks d4.} Bd7 {Breaks the pin.}
6. Bxc6 {White gives up the bishop so the knight can't take on d4 or e5.}
(6. Nf3? {Natural, but the pawn on e5 is now weak.} Nxe5! {The pin is broken, so the knight simply takes.} 7. Nxe5 Bxb5 {Black has won a pawn, and the bishop on b5 stops White from castling. Stockfish −1.6, clearly better for Black.})
6... bxc6 {Toward the center. The pawn on c6 supports d5, and the c-file opens for Black's rook.}
7. Nf3 cxd4 8. cxd4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 again. White has given up the light-squared bishop, so the light squares around White's king are weak.}
9. O-O cxd4 10. Nbd2 Ne7 11. Nb3 Ng6 12. Qxd4 Qb6 {White wins back the pawn, and the queens will come off. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Advance: sixth moves after 5...cxd4, 5...Bd7 or 5...Nge7"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "6...Bb4+"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Paulsen Attack"]
[ECO "C02"]

{Black's other sixth moves after 5...cxd4, 5...Bd7 and 5...Nge7. White's plans stay the same: hold d4 with pieces, castle, and gain space.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} cxd4
(5... Bd7 {This is the Euwe Variation.} 6. Be2 Nge7 7. O-O Ng6 8. Be3 {Guards d4 a third time.} Be7 9. Nbd2 {White has a comfortable space advantage. Stockfish +0.2, equal.})
(5... Nge7 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Nf5 8. Bxf5 {Removes the knight that attacks d4.} exf5 9. Nc3 h6 10. h4 {[%csl Yf5] Black's doubled f-pawns block the bishop on c8. Stockfish +0.2, equal.})
6. cxd4 Bb4+ {The check looks active, but it helps White develop.}
7. Nc3 f6 {Black tries to undermine e5.} 8. Bd3 fxe5 9. dxe5 {White's pieces are more active, and Black's e6 pawn is weak. Stockfish +1.1, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Advance: Black's other fourth and fifth moves"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...c4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation"]
[ECO "C02"]

{Rarer tries by Black after 4.c3. Against 4...Bd7, play 5.Nf3: after 5...Nc6 you are in the 5...Bd7 chapter. Against 5...a6, play 6.Bd3 and castle. Against 5...Qa5, take on c5 with gain of time: 6.dxc5 Qxc5 7.Bd3 and castle (Stockfish +0.8).}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 c4 {Locks the queenside early, but takes the pressure off d4.}
5. b3 {[%cal Gb3c4] Attacks the chain at once.} b5 6. a4 Qa5 7. Bb2 Nc6 8. axb5 Qxb5 9. Nd2 {The b-file opens and Black's pawn on c4 becomes a target. Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 4.Bd3, 4.Nc3 and 5.Bb5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "C01"]

{White's other fourth and fifth moves in the Exchange. The plan doesn't change: ...Nc6, ...Bd6, a knight on e7, and short castling. If White stops ...Bg4 with h3, trade the light-squared bishops with ...Bf5 instead. Against 4.Nc3 the knight belongs on f6, where it guards d5, and against 5.Bb5, develop with ...Bd6 and ...Nge7.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4
(2. Nc3 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. d4 Nc6 {The same position as the 4.Nc3 line below.})
(2. Nf3 {After 2.d4, the most common second move.} d5 3. exd5 {Most players at this level take at once.} exd5 4. d4 Nc6 {The same position as the Exchange main line after 4.Nf3 Nc6. White's other tries after 2.Nf3 are under Knight Variation.})
2... d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. Bd3
(4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Bb5 {Pins the knight and threatens to double Black's pawns.} Bd6 {Develops and prepares to castle. If White takes on c6, the pawn on c6 supports d5.} 6. O-O Nge7 7. c3 O-O 8. Re1 Ng6 {Next ...Bg4 or ...Bf5. Equal (Stockfish +0.1).})
(4. Nc3 {Develops and attacks d5.} Nc6 {After 5.Bb5, pin back with 5...Bb4.} 5. Nf3 Nf6 {[%cal Gf6d5] Guards d5, which White's knight attacks. Don't play 5...Bd6?, which drops the pawn to 6.Nxd5 (Stockfish +1.8).} 6. Bb5 Bb4 {Pin for pin.} 7. O-O O-O 8. Bxc6 bxc6 9. Ne5 Bxc3 10. bxc3 Re8 {Both sides have damaged pawns. Equal (Stockfish +0.2).})
4... Nc6 5. c3 {After 5.Nf3 Bg4, you are back in the Exchange main line.} Bd6
6. h3 {Stops ...Bg4. After 6.Nf3 Bg4, you are in the Exchange main line again. After 6.Ne2, play 6...Nge7 and castle.} Nge7 7. Nf3 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5d3] Offers to trade White's good bishop.}
8. Bxf5 Nxf5 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 Nce7 {The knight makes room for ...c6, which makes d5 rock solid.} 11. Nbd2 c6 {Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Winawer: 7...O-O"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "7...O-O"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Winawer Variation, Warsaw Variation"]
[ECO "C18"]

{Castling guards g7 without giving up a pawn, but Black has to defend for a while. White aims at h7 with Bd3, Qh5 and Nf3, and Black keeps the kingside closed. Later Black closes the queenside with ...c4 and plays there.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Ne7 7. Qg4 O-O {Black guards g7 by castling. Solid, but passive.}
8. Bd3 Nbc6 9. Qh5 {[%cal Gh5h7,Gd3h7] Two pieces aim at h7.} Ng6 {The knight shields h7 and the kingside.}
10. Nf3 Qc7 11. Be3 c4 {[%cal Gc4d3] Hits the bishop on d3 and closes the queenside. Slightly better for White (Stockfish +0.5).} *

[Chapter "Tarrasch: 3...c5"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "3...c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Tarrasch Variation, Chistyakov Defense, Modern Line"]
[ECO "C07"]

{The open variation: Black hits d4 at once, and after 4.exd5 recaptures with the queen, so the pawn on e6 stays to guard Black's king. White wins the d4 pawn back with Ngf3, Bc4 and Nb3, and Black gets free development; ...a6 keeps White's pieces off b5. Taking back with 4...exd5 is also playable, but it usually leaves Black with an isolated d-pawn.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 {The open variation: Black strikes at d4 at once.}
4. exd5 Qxd5 {The queen recaptures, and the pawn on e6 keeps guarding Black's king.} 5. Ngf3 cxd4 6. Bc4 {Gains time on the queen.} Qd6 {Not all the way back to d8.}
7. O-O Nf6 8. Nb3 {[%cal Gb3d4,Gf3d4] A third attacker on d4: the pawn will fall.} Nc6 {Black gives the pawn back. Holding it with ...e5 leaves Black's king stuck in the center.}
9. Nbxd4 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 a6 {Keeps White's pieces off b5.}
11. Re1 Be7 {Black castles next, with free pieces. Slightly better for White (Stockfish +0.3).} *

[Chapter "Steinitz: 7...Qb6"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "7...Qb6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Steinitz Variation, Boleslavsky Variation"]
[ECO "C11"]

{7...Qb6 attacks b2 and leads to a forcing line. After 8.Na4 Qa5+ 9.c3 cxd4 10.b4, Black gives a knight for two pawns with 10...Nxb4, and White's king stays in the center for a while. It is sharp and risky for Black: at the end Stockfish prefers White (+1.5), so play it only if you know it well.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Qb6 {[%cal Gb6b2] Hits b2.}
8. Na4 {Hits the queen and guards b2.} Qa5+ 9. c3 cxd4 10. b4 {Attacks the queen again.} Nxb4 {A knight for two pawns.}
11. cxb4 Bxb4+ 12. Bd2 Bxd2+ 13. Nxd2 O-O 14. Bd3 b6 {The bishop comes to a6 next. Clearly better for White (Stockfish +1.5), but Black has two pawns and play against White's king.} *

[Chapter "Steinitz: 7...cxd4"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "7...cxd4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Steinitz Variation, Boleslavsky Variation"]
[ECO "C11"]

{7...cxd4 releases the tension at once. After 8.Nxd4 Bc5 9.Qd2 O-O 10.O-O-O, the kings are on opposite sides and both sides attack. Against 11.h4, Black trades on d4 and races with ...b5 and ...b4.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4
8. Nxd4 Bc5 {[%cal Gc5d4] More pressure on d4. Black doesn't take on d4 yet.} 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O {Opposite-side castling: both sides attack.} a6 {Keeps White's knights off b5 and prepares ...b5.}
11. h4 {White's best try: the kingside pawns go forward at once.} Nxd4 12. Bxd4 b5 13. h5 {The h-pawn keeps going. 13.Rh3, bringing the rook to the third rank, is also played, but Stockfish prefers 13.h5.} b4
14. Na4 Bxd4 15. Qxd4 {Equal (Stockfish +0.1). White's attack is easy to play, but the knight on a4 is offside.} *

[Chapter "Steinitz: 7...Be7"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "7...Be7"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Steinitz Variation, Boleslavsky Variation"]
[ECO "C11"]

{7...Be7 looks quiet, but it sets a trap: after 8.Qd2 O-O, White must not castle queenside. The normal plan is Be2 and short castling. Black plays ...b6 and then ...f6 to break in the center.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7
8. Qd2 O-O 9. Be2 {Not 9.O-O-O?, which runs into 9...c4! and ...b5 (Stockfish −1.5).} b6 {Prepares ...Bb7 or ...Ba6.}
10. O-O f6 {[%cal Gf6e5] Black's second break, against the head of the chain.} 11. dxc5 Nxc5 12. Rad1 Bb7 13. Qe1 fxe5 14. fxe5 {[%csl Ye5,Ye4] The pawn on e5 is isolated, and e4 is a good square for Black's knight. Slightly better for White (Stockfish +0.3).} *

[Chapter "Burn Variation: 4.Bg5 dxe4"]
[Side "both"]
[Diagram "4...dxe4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Classical Variation, Burn Variation, Morozevich Line"]
[ECO "C13"]

{Against 4.Bg5, Black takes on e4 and recaptures on f6 with the g-pawn: the g-file opens for Black's rook, and Black keeps the dark-squared bishop. 6...Bxf6 is the more common, quieter recapture. Both sides often castle queenside.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 {Pins the knight against the queen.} dxe4 5. Nxe4 Be7 {Breaks the pin.}
6. Bxf6 gxf6 {[%cal Gh8g8] Opens the g-file for Black's rook.} 7. Nf3 f5 {Drives the knight away from the center.}
8. Nc3 a6 9. Qd2 {White prepares long castling. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Rubinstein: 4...Nf6 and ...gxf6"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4...Nf6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Rubinstein Variation"]
[ECO "C10"]

{A sharp alternative to 4...Nd7: after 5.Nxf6+ gxf6, Black's pawns are damaged, but Black gets the half-open g-file, a long diagonal for the c8 bishop, and a pawn on f6 that keeps White's pieces off e5. The plan is ...b6 and ...Bb7, ...Rg8, then ...Qd6 and long castling. It is riskier than 4...Nd7: Stockfish rates the main line slightly better for White (+0.9, against +0.3 for 4...Nd7). Use it as a surprise, especially in faster games. Against 5.Bg5, which is just as common, play 5...Be7, and after 6.Nxf6+ recapture with the bishop.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 {This is the Paulsen Variation.} dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {Challenges the knight at once.}
5. Nxf6+ gxf6 {[%cal Gh8g8] Opens the g-file.}
6. Nf3 b6 {[%cal Gc8b7] The French bishop goes to the long diagonal, toward White's kingside.}
7. Bc4 Bb7 8. O-O Rg8 {[%cal Gg8g2] The rook takes the g-file.}
9. g3 Qd6 {Next ...Nd7 and long castling, then ...f5 and ...Rg4 or a push of the h-pawn. Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White, but the position is sharp and easy to misplay for White.} *

[Chapter "Rubinstein: 3.Nd2, 5.Bd3 and other sixth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Bg5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Rubinstein Variation, Blackburne Defense"]
[ECO "C10"]

{After 4...Nd7 5.Nf3 Ngf6, White has three other sixth moves. The plan stays the same: trade knights or bishops, then free the game with ...c5 or ...e5.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 {This is the Paulsen Variation.}
(3. Nd2 {The Tarrasch. The knight also defends e4.} dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {This is the Blackburne Defense of the Rubinstein Variation. The same position as after 3.Nc3: one system answers both knight moves, as in the Rubinstein lesson.})
3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {This is the Blackburne Defense of the Rubinstein Variation.} 5. Nf3
(5. Bd3 {Develops the bishop first.} Ngf6 6. Nf3 {The same position as the main line after 6.Bd3. After 6.Nxf6+ Nxf6 7.Nf3 c5, you also reach the main line.})
5... Ngf6
6. Bg5 {Pins the knight against the queen.}
(6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 7. Bd3 c5 {The same position as the main line after 8...c5.})
(6. Ng3 {Keeps the knights on the board.} c5 {Hits d4 at once.} 7. c3 Be7 {Black develops and castles next. Equal (Stockfish −0.2).})
6... Be7 {Breaks the pin. Against 7.Bxf6, take back with the knight; against 7.Bd3, play 7...c5.}
7. Nxf6+ Bxf6 {Recapture with the bishop: Black gets the bishop pair.}
8. h4 {Supports the bishop on g5 and prepares long castling.} O-O 9. Qd2 e5! {[%cal Ge6e5] The second freeing break. It hits d4 and opens the diagonal of the c8 bishop.}
10. Bxf6 Qxf6 11. O-O-O exd4 12. Qxd4 Qxd4 13. Rxd4 Nf6 {Equal endgame (Stockfish 0.0). Black's bishop is no longer bad.} *

[Chapter "Franco-Sicilian: 2...c5 and other second moves"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "2...c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Franco-Sicilian Defense"]
[ECO "C00"]

{Some players answer 2.d4 without 2...d5. Together these moves come up in about one game in six. Take the center and develop; White is better against all of them.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 c5 {This is the Franco-Sicilian Defense. A Sicilian-like move.}
(2... b6 {The bishop goes to b7.} 3. Nf3 Bb7 4. Bd3 c5 5. c3 {Keeps the pawn chain on c3 and d4.} Nf6 6. Qe2 {Guards e4. Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White.})
(2... d6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Bd3 c5 5. c3 {White has the center and easy development. Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White.})
(2... c6 3. Nf3 d5 4. e5 c5 5. c3 Nc6 {The Advance French, but Black has spent two moves on the c-pawn.} 6. Bd3 Nge7 7. O-O {An extra tempo for White in a known structure. Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White.})
3. d5 {Gains space. The pawn on d5 cramps Black.} Nf6 4. Nc3 d6 5. dxe6 Bxe6 6. Nge2 {Heads for f4 to hit the bishop on e6.} Nc6 7. Nf4 {Black's d6 pawn is weak. Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Knight Variation: other third moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "3.e5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Knight Variation"]
[ECO "C00"]

{After 2.Nf3 d5, most White players take on d5 and play d4, which is the Exchange. Here are the other tries. Against 3.e5, play 3...c5 and ...Nc6: once White plays d4, it is the Advance. If White takes on d5 and then doesn't play d4, develop with ...Nf6, ...Bd6 and ...O-O; the position is equal.}
1. e4 e6 2. Nf3 d5
3. e5 {The Advance without d4 yet.}
(3. Nc3 {The Two Knights.} Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. d4 c5 {Hits d4, as in the Advance. With the knight on c3, White can't support d4 with c3, so the pressure is strong.} 6. Ne2 Nc6 {Equal (Stockfish +0.1).})
3... c5
4. c3 {Prepares d4.}
(4. d4 {This is the Nimzowitsch System.} Nc6 {The Advance with 4.Nf3: after 5.c3 Qb6 we are in the main line.})
(4. b4 {The Wing Gambit. White offers the b-pawn to pull Black's c-pawn away from d4.} cxb4 {Take it.} 5. d4 {After 5.a3, take again with 5...bxa3 and develop.} Bd7 {[%cal Gd7b5] Heads for b5 to trade the French bishop.} 6. a3 Qa5 7. Bd3 Bb5 {Trades the bishops and keeps the extra pawn. Slightly better for Black (Stockfish −0.4).})
4... Nc6 5. d4 {This is the Paulsen Attack. After 5.Bb5, play 5...Bd7.} Qb6 {The main line of the Advance after 5...Qb6, by another move order.} *

[Chapter "Rare second moves: 2.Bc4, 2.c4 and 2.d3"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "2.Bc4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[ECO "C00"]

{Against White's rarer second moves, play ...d5 and develop: most lead to open positions where Black's pieces come out easily. After 2.Nc3 d5 3.exd5 exd5 4.d4, you are in the Exchange with 4.Nc3. After 2.e5, play 2...d5, and if 3.d4 c5, it is the Advance. Against 2.Qe2, play 2...c5 and ...Nc6, keeping the queen's pawn back.}
1. e4 e6
2. Bc4 {Aims at f7.}
(2. c4 {The Steiner Variation.} d5 3. cxd5 {After 3.exd5 exd5, develop with ...Nf6. Against 4.Qb3, play 4...dxe4 5.Bc4 Qe7 (Stockfish −1.0); against 4.e5, play 4...d4.} exd5 4. exd5 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nxd5 {Black wins back the pawn with an easy game. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).})
(2. d3 {The King's Indian Attack. White plans Nd2, Ngf3, g3, Bg2 and O-O, then e5 or a kingside attack.} d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. Ngf3 Nc6 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 Be7 7. O-O O-O {Black has a good share of the center. The plan is ...b5 and ...b4 on the queenside. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).})
2... d5 {Attacks the bishop: White has to take or retreat.}
3. exd5 exd5 4. Bb3 Nf6 5. d4 Bd6 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O h6 {Black has an easy game. Equal (Stockfish −0.1).} *

[Chapter "La Bourdonnais Variation: 2.f4"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: La Bourdonnais Variation"]
[ECO "C00"]

{2.f4 supports a later e5 push and prepares Nf3. It also weakens White's kingside. Play 2...d5. Against 3.e5, use the Advance plan: ...c5, ...Nc6 and a knight on f5. Against 3.exd5, recapture and develop; Black is already slightly better.}
1. e4 e6 2. f4 {This is the La Bourdonnais Variation.} d5
3. e5
(3. exd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Nf6 {Black develops freely, and f4 has weakened e4 and the diagonal to White's king. Slightly better for Black (Stockfish −0.6).})
3... c5 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. c3 {After 5.Bb5, play 5...Bd7.} Nge7 {[%cal Ge7f5] Heads for f5. White's pawn on f4 can't kick it from there.}
6. d4 cxd4 7. cxd4 Nf5 {[%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4] Two knights hit d4. Slightly better for Black (Stockfish −0.4).} *

[Chapter "Horwitz: 2.b3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "French Defense: Horwitz Attack"]
[ECO "C00"]

{GM Igor Smirnov recommends 2.b3 as a surprise: the bishop on b2 aims at Black's kingside. Black's simplest answer is ...d5 and ...Nc6. If White pushes e5, ...d4 blocks the bishop's diagonal for good.}
1. e4 e6 2. b3 d5 3. Bb2 Nc6 {Develops and prepares ...d4. Taking on e4 is also playable, but it gives White the quick development the gambit wants.}
4. exd5 (4. e5 d4 {[%csl Rb2] Shuts in the bishop on b2. Slightly better for Black (Stockfish −0.3).}) exd5
5. Nf3 Bg4 6. Be2 Nf6 7. O-O Bd6 {Black's pieces are well placed, and the knight on f6 blunts the bishop on b2. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Trap: the Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "6.Bd3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Milner-Barry Gambit, Main Line"]
[ECO "C02"]

{A risky surprise weapon: with 6.Bd3, White offers the d4 pawn for quick development and an attack. Against best play Black keeps the pawn and is slightly better (Stockfish −0.6), so use it only when you want a sharp game. But Black often grabs too much or too early: 7...Nxd4?? loses the queen, and 10...Qxe5 has its own trap chapter. The gambit is named after Stuart Milner-Barry, an English player and wartime codebreaker.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Qb6
6. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3b5] The bishop now stands between the queen and d4, so White is offering the d4 pawn.}
6... cxd4
(6... Bd7 {The most common reply at club level.} 7. dxc5 {Take the pawn: the bishop on d7 can't defend c5.} Bxc5 8. O-O a5 9. Qe2 {White keeps e5 and is ahead in development. Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White.})
7. cxd4 Bd7 {The best defense: it covers b5 first.}
(7... Nxd4?? {Takes the bait.} 8. Nxd4 Qxd4? {Black should give up the knight with 8...Bc5 instead.} 9. Bb5+ {The bishop moves off the d-file with check, and White's queen takes Black's queen next move. Stockfish +9.3, winning for White.})
(7... Bb4+? {Checks, but blocks nothing.} 8. Nc3 Bd7 9. Be3 Nge7 10. O-O {White keeps d4, and Black's bishop on b4 has no good square. Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White.})
8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 {White is a pawn down but ready for Nb5 and Re1.} a6 {Stops Nb5. The correct defense.}
11. Re1 Ne7 12. g3 Qb6 13. a3 Rc8 {Black is a pawn up and solid. Stockfish −0.6, slightly better for Black: this is the price of the gambit.} *

[Chapter "Trap: defusing the Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Bd3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Milner-Barry Gambit, Main Line"]
[ECO "C02"]

{6.Bd3 is White's most common answer to 5...Qb6: White offers the d4 pawn as a gambit, and there is a trap behind it. The gambit is named after Stuart Milner-Barry, an English player who was a codebreaker at Bletchley Park during the Second World War. The rule: play ...Bd7 before ...Nxd4, and then the pawn is really yours, with Black slightly better.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Qb6
6. Bd3 {The Milner-Barry Gambit.} cxd4 {Stockfish's choice. The more common 6...Bd7 allows 7.dxc5! Bxc5 8.O-O, and White is slightly better (+0.5).}
7. cxd4
(7. O-O {The Hector line: White doesn't recapture at all.} Bd7 8. Re1 Nge7 9. h4 h6 {Black stops h5–h6 and keeps the extra pawn. Equal (Stockfish +0.2).})
7... Bd7 {[%cal Rd3b5,Rb5e8] Covers the b5–e8 diagonal first. Now ...Nxd4 really wins a pawn.}
(7... Nxd4?? {The trap.} 8. Nxd4 Qxd4? 9. Bb5+ {The bishop moves off the d-file with check, and White's queen takes Black's queen next move. Stockfish +9.3, winning for White.})
8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4
10. Nc3 {White has two pieces out and wants Nb5 or Re1.} a6 {[%cal Ga6b5] Stops Nb5, which would hit the queen and c7.}
(10... Qxe5?! {Greedy.} 11. Re1 Qd6 12. Nb5 Qb6 {The only move: 12...Bxb5? 13.Bxb5+ and 12...Qb8? 13.Qf3 both lose. After 13.Be3 Qa5 14.Bd2 Qb6, White can repeat moves, so Black gets nothing. Stockfish 0.0.})
11. Re1 {After 11.Qe2, play 11...Ne7 too.} Ne7 12. g3 Qb6 13. a3 Rc8 {Black is a pawn up with a solid position. Slightly better for Black (Stockfish −0.6).} *

[Chapter "Trap: the e5 pawn grab"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "10...Qxe5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Milner-Barry Gambit, Main Line"]
[ECO "C02"]

{In the Milner-Barry Gambit, Black grabs the e5 pawn as well in about half of all games. 11.Re1 brings the rook to the e-file with gain of time, and Black's king is stuck in the center. Only one defense holds.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 Qxe5
11. Re1 {[%cal Re1e5,Re5e8] Hits the queen on the e-file. After 11...Qc7 12.Nxd5, White is slightly better; after 11...Qb8, play 12.Nxd5 too.} Qd6
12. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5d6,Gb5c7] Hits the queen again and eyes c7.} Qb8? {Natural, but too slow.}
(12... Bxb5? 13. Bxb5+ {Black's king can't castle and has no shelter. Stockfish +2.6, winning for White.})
(12... Qc6? 13. Bf4! {Threatens Nc7+, forking the king and the rook on a8.} Rc8 14. Nxa7 {Wins the exchange. Stockfish +6.9, winning for White.})
(12... Qb6 {The only defense.} 13. Be3 Qa5 14. Bd2 Qb6 {White can repeat moves with 15.Be3. Stockfish 0.0, equal.})
13. Qf3 {[%cal Gf3f7,Gf3b7] The queen joins the attack.} Bd6 14. Nxd6+ Qxd6 15. Bf4 {Black's queen is hit again, and White's rooks come to c1 and e1. Stockfish +4.4, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the Monte Carlo, 4.c4"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.c4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "French Defense: Exchange Variation, Monte Carlo Variation"]
[ECO "C01"]

{4.c4 hits d5 from the side. GM Igor Smirnov calls it a secret weapon: after 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 Bb4 6.Nf3 O-O 7.Bd3, Black's natural attacking moves backfire. Our move order avoids his traps: check first with ...Bb4+, castle, and take on c4 only after White's bishop has moved, so White spends two moves on it.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5
4. c4 Bb4+ {Develops with check. White has to block.}
5. Nc3
(5. Bd2 Bxd2+ 6. Nxd2 {After 6.Qxd2 Nf6 7.Nf3 O-O, Black is just as comfortable.} Nf6 7. Ngf3 O-O 8. Be2 Bg4 9. O-O Nc6 {Equal (Stockfish 0.0).})
5... Nf6 6. Nf3 {After 6.Bg5 or 6.a3, play 6...O-O; against 6.a3, take on c3 first.} O-O
7. Bd3 {Smirnov's move. The more common 7.Be2 is met the same way: 7...dxc4 8.Bxc4.} dxc4 {Now that White's bishop has moved, it must move again to take back.}
(7... Re8+?! {Smirnov's trap starts here.} 8. Be3 Ng4? 9. O-O! Nxe3 10. fxe3 Rxe3?? {Takes the pawn, but...} 11. Nxd5 {The knight forks the rook on e3 and the bishop on b4. Stockfish +6.8, winning for White.})
8. Bxc4 Bg4 {Pins the knight that defends d4.}
(8... Re8+?! 9. Be3 Ng4? 10. O-O Nxe3? 11. Bxf7+! {Smirnov's other trick: after 11...Kxf7 12.fxe3, White's rook on f1 and queen join the attack on Black's king. Stockfish +2.5, winning for White.})
9. O-O Nc6 10. Be3 Qd6 {White has an isolated d-pawn. Black blockades it with a knight on d5 later and trades pieces. Equal (Stockfish −0.1).} *

[Chapter "Trap: the early ...Nb3"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "8...Nb3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Main Line"]
[ECO "C02"]

{After 6.a3 c4, Black's knight heads for b3. Jumping in too early drops a pawn: White trades on b3, and the pawn that recaptures there falls.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 {This is the Paulsen Attack.} Qb6 6. a3 c4 7. Nbd2 Na5 8. Rb1 Nb3? {Looks strong: the knight hits the bishop on c1, the knight on d2 and the pawn on d4. But White can simply take it.}
9. Nxb3 Qxb3 10. Qxb3 cxb3 11. Nd2 {[%csl Rb3] The pawn on b3 has no support.} Bd7 12. Nxb3 {White is a pawn up. Stockfish +1.9, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 3...f6 and Qh5+"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "3...f6"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation"]
[ECO "C02"]

{Some players break at e5 with 3...f6 at once. 4.Bd3 aims at g6 and prepares Qh5+. If Black takes on e5, the queen check wins material.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 f6 4. Bd3 {[%cal Gd1h5,Gd3g6] Prepares Qh5+.} fxe5?
(4... g6 {Stops Qh5+, but weakens the dark squares.} 5. h4 {Attacks the g6 pawn with h5. Stockfish +1.3, clearly better for White.})
5. Qh5+ g6 6. Bxg6+ {6...hxg6?? 7.Qxh8 wins the rook.} Kd7 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. Nxe5+ Nxe5 9. Qxe5 {White is a pawn up and Black's king is stuck in the center. Stockfish +2.2, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 3.f3 and ...Qh4+"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "3.f3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "French Defense"]
[ECO "C00"]

{3.f3 is rare, but when White plays it, the natural recapture 4.fxe4 runs into a queen check. Know the trick.}
1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5
3. f3 {Supports e4 with a pawn, but weakens the diagonal from h4 to White's king.} dxe4 {Take. The natural recapture is a mistake.}
4. fxe4
(4. Nc3 {White's best: develop and offer the pawn.} Bb4 {Pins the knight. If White takes on e4, the queen check still comes.} 5. fxe4 Qh4+ 6. Ke2 {6.g3? Qxe4+ also attacks the rook on h1.} Bxc3 7. bxc3 Qxe4+ 8. Kf2 Qh4+ 9. g3 Qd8 {Black is a pawn up, and White's king has moved. Slightly better for Black (Stockfish −0.6).})
4... Qh4+ {[%cal Gh4e1,Gh4e4] Checks the king and hits e4.}
5. Kd2
(5. g3? Qxe4+ {Forks the king and the rook on h1.} 6. Qe2 Qxh1 {Black has won a rook. Stockfish −6.8, winning for Black.})
5... Qxe4 {Black has won a pawn, and White's king is stuck in the center.}
6. Nf3 Bd6 {Next ...Nc6 and ...Nf6. Stockfish −1.9, clearly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Map of the variations"]
[Type "map"]

1. e4 e6 {French Defense} 2. d4 d5
3. e5 {[%course both] Advance Variation}
(3. exd5 exd5 {[%course both] Exchange Variation})
(3. Nc3 Bb4 {[%course both] Winawer Variation})
(3. Nd2 {[%course both] Tarrasch Variation})
(3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 {[%course both] Steinitz Variation})
(3. Nc3 dxe4 {[%course both] Rubinstein Variation}) *
